{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And the children of Israel came into the midst of the sea.</b> Do not think, because Scripture says 'into the midst of the sea,' that they reached the middle of the Sea of Reeds. Rather, even if they entered half a parseh into the sea, it is called 'the midst of the sea,' as in 'and the riffraff that were in the midst of it' (Numbers 11:4) — and they were not in the middle of the camp. The sense of 'into the midst of the sea' is the place where there had been sea at the beginning of the night." "<b>And the waters were a wall to them.</b> For the waters congealed, and it is written, 'they stood like a heap of flowing waters' (below, 15:8). And after most of Israel had passed through, the water that had been a wall to them melted — this is 'and the waters returned upon Egypt' (below, v. 26) — and they separated between the Egyptians and the dry land; therefore it is written, 'and the Egyptians pursued after it' (v. 27), for they thought they would emerge onto dry land, and the walls of water had melted and cut them off from the dry land. And the pursuers undoubtedly saw the light, how Israel was passing through."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:34:31.814996080Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}